The only non-trivial part was matching [38;5;10mscanf[0m rather than reading lines. I wrote a byte reader with single-byte pushback so it reproduces glibc's behavior:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- whitespace skipping spans newlines, and both [38;5;10mscanf[0m calls are independent[0m[0m
- return values are ignored in the C, so a failed conversion leaves [38;5;10mx[0m/[38;5;10my[0m at their initializer [38;5;10m0[0m (empty stdin therefore prints [38;5;10m-1[0m, since [38;5;10m0 | ~0 == -1[0m)[0m[0m
- an optional sign is consumed even when no digit follows, and only the single offending byte is pushed back — so [38;5;10m"- 5"[0m fails the first conversion but the second still reads [38;5;10m5[0m[0m[0m
- out-of-range digit strings follow glibc's [38;5;10mstrtol[0m clamp to [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m followed by narrowing to [38;5;10mint[0m, which is why [38;5;10m"99999999999999999999"[0m yields [38;5;10m-1[0m rather than a saturated [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m[0m[0m
